[0.053,"o","\u001b[H\u001b[48;5;237m                                                                                          \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mThe aging report tells me how much is overdue, but not which invoices, so I end up \u001b[39m     \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231msearching the JSON file. Add \u001b[38;5;153m--details\u001b[38;5;231m to \u001b[38;5;153minvoicer aging\u001b[38;5;231m.\u001b[39m                               \r\u001b[1B                                                                                          \r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;231mAcceptance criteria:\u001b[39m                                                                    \r\u001b[1B                              \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;231m1. With \u001b[38;5;153m--details\u001b[38;5;231m, each bucket's row is followed by its invoices, one per line: the \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1Binvoice's\u001b[14Gd, th\u001b[20G customer's name, the due date, the days overdue and the amount.\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B2. Within a bucket, the invoice overdue the longest comes first.\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B3. The amounts are the\u001b[26Ginvoices' totals, printed as the report pr\u001b[69Gnt\u001b[72G am\u001b[76Gunts, and\u001b[86Gthey\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1Badd up to\u001b[17Gbucket's amount.\u001b[39m                           \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;231m4. Without \u001b[38;5;153m--details\u001b[38;5;231m, \u001b[38;5;153minvoicer aging\u001b[38;5;231m prints exactly what it prints today.\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m\u001b[49m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m I'll start by exploring the codebase to understand the existing aging report.\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;246m●\u001b[39m Finding aging usages and configs\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;246m  ⎿  \u001b[39m\u001b[2m\u001b[3mPlan: - aging.ts: add \u001b[38;5;153mdays\u001b[39m to bucket invoices. Introduce \u001b[38;5;153mAgingInvoice { invoice: \u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B     \u001b[38;5;153m\u001b[2m\u001b[3mInvoice; days: number }\u001b[39m? But criterion 3: \"amounts are the invoices' totals\" — \u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B     \u001b[2m\u001b[3mcomputed in renderer via totals(invoice).total. Should details amount be rounded \u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B     \u001b[2m\u001b[3minvoice total, and bucket amount sumAmounts. Criterion 3 \"they add up to the bucket's\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bamount\" — sum of displayed invoice totals should equal bucket amount. Since bucket \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m \u001b[3G   \u001b[2m\u001b[3mamount = sumAmounts(totals), and each total already rounded, sumAmounts of those \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bequals bucket amount. So display formatMoney(totals(i).total). Good. - Sort \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m \u001b[3G   \u001b[2m\u001b[3mbucket.invoices by days descending. Since invoices are pushed in order of source \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m     \u001b[2m\u001b[3marray which may not be sorted. Criterion 2. Could sort the invoices array in aging \u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m\u001b[3mdirectly, so even without details order irrelevant but consistent. Sorting…\u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;174m✢\u001b[3GEncha\u001b[38;5;216mnti\u001b[38;5;174mng… \u001b[38;5;246m(36s · ↓\u001b[24G5.3k tokens · thought for 13s)\r\u001b[1B  ⎿  Tip: Use /btw\u001b[20Gto ask a\u001b[29Gquick side question without interrupting Claude's current work\u001b[39m\u001b[34;1H\u001b[32;3H"]
